// RUN: %not %bezelc -c %s 2>&1 | FileCheck %s
//
// (`%not` = LLVM's exit-status inverter; the compile is expected to fail — see
// tests/lit/README.md for why every reject test wraps `bezelc` in `not`.)
//
// Issue #1730 — self-import must be a compile error (spec §2.8.1 rule 4,
// v0.188).
//
// Spec §2.8.1 rule 4: "An `import` naming the enclosing file's own module is
// rejected ('a module cannot import itself'). Import cycles between distinct
// modules remain legal and compile as an SCC (§2.5); the self-loop is not a
// cycle, it is a mistake." Before this fix the compiler silently ACCEPTED
// `module SelfImp; import SelfImp;` (proposal 0034 §1's evidence probe) — no
// diagnostic at all. This is repro shape 1 from #1730: a self-import with NO
// dependency artifact supplied. See self_import_dup_artifact_1730.bz for
// shape 2 (a same-named artifact IS supplied — the compiler used to invent a
// third, nowhere-specified semantics instead of rejecting).
module SelfImp;
import SelfImp;

class Program
  public static method Main(): int return 0; end Main;
end Program;

// bezelc emits diagnostics as `file:line:col: message` with no "error:"
// severity word, so anchor on the `:line:col:` shape and the rule text.
// CHECK: :{{[0-9]+}}:{{[0-9]+}}: module `SelfImp` cannot import itself
// CHECK-SAME: (§2.8.1 rule 4)
